## What Are Champagne Nails, Exactly?

Champagne nails are a warm, low-saturation neutral that sits between nude and gold, built on a sheer pink-beige base with a metallic or pearl shimmer running through it. The finish is what separates it from a flat neutral. Where a plain nude polish stops at color, champagne adds light.

Nude nails are matte or satin and skin-toned, with no shimmer built in. Champagne always carries some reflective quality, whether that is a soft pearl sheen or a full chrome powder finish, and it leans pinker and cooler than a straight gold, which reads more amber and metallic with none of the underlying blush. Think of nude as the base note and champagne as that same note with a finish layered over it.

The name comes directly from the drink, and the comparison holds up under scrutiny. Champagne in a glass is not clear and it is not gold. It is pale, warm, faintly pink, catching light in a way water never does. That is the effect behind every version of <strong>champagne nails new years</strong> revelers keep asking for, whichever finish they end up choosing.

